  {
   "id": "4QH4h1j6AwAJ",
   "from": "Lucas Deschamps",
   "author": "a3d13f141d9746dc",
   "date": "2018-03-06T18:53:45Z",
   "subject": "Reparametrization of gamma distribution",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello!<div><br></div><div>I&#39;m used to model jointly mean and dispersion for strictly positive variables. To do this, I describe response variable by a gamma distribution parametrized by the two orthogonal terms mu (mean) and phi (precision). That is, in stan code :</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">  shape </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">.*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">./</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  rate </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">./</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code><br style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\">     y ~ gamma(shape, rate); </span></div><div><br></div></div><div>Because I have to use splines to produce a GAMM for a particular problem, I found far more convenient to use brms instead of rstan. Unfortunatly, the gamma disribution is only parametrized in term of mu and shape, with the following formulation I found in the code created by brms : </div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><pre style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:19px;font-variant-numeric:normal;font-variant-east-asian:normal;font-size:12.4pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><span style=\"outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> shape</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> exp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(-(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]));</span></font></span></pre></div></code></div><br>However, in that case, the precision is not independant of the mean.</div><div><br></div><div>Is there any trick that would allow me to use the formulation with mu and phi, but also to keep the advantages provided by brmsfit objects??</div><div><br></div><div>Thank you very much!</div><div>Lucas</div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "OGZgQK0cAAAJ",
   "from": "Paul Buerkner",
   "author": "6e3dd74b422c061b",
   "date": "2018-03-06T18:58:20Z",
   "subject": "Re: [brms-users] Reparametrization of gamma distribution",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>No solution that I am aware of. In the parameterization I use in brms, mu is the mean, but as you say, the precision depends on the mean.<br><br></div>The only solution would be to add a new family, gamma2() say, that uses your parameterization. You may comment on <a href=\"https://github.com/paul-buerkner/brms/issues/110\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/paul-buerkner/brms/issues/110&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451331100000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0PQbKniEDV8iynL1NpiXfh\">https://github.com/paul-buerkner/brms/issues/110</a> so that I don&#39;t lose track of it.<br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">2018-03-06 19:53 GMT+01:00 Lucas Deschamps <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">lucas.de...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span>:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hello!<div><br></div><div>I&#39;m used to model jointly mean and dispersion for strictly positive variables. To do this, I describe response variable by a gamma distribution parametrized by the two orthogonal terms mu (mean) and phi (precision). That is, in stan code :</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">  shape </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">.*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">./</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  rate </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">./</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code><br style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\">     y ~ gamma(shape, rate); </span></div><div><br></div></div><div>Because I have to use splines to produce a GAMM for a particular problem, I found far more convenient to use brms instead of rstan. Unfortunatly, the gamma disribution is only parametrized in term of mu and shape, with the following formulation I found in the code created by brms : </div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><pre style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:19px;font-variant-numeric:normal;font-variant-east-asian:normal;font-size:12.4pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><span style=\"outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> shape</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> exp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(-(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]));</span></font></span></pre></div></code></div><br>However, in that case, the precision is not independant of the mean.</div><div><br></div><div>Is there any trick that would allow me to use the formulation with mu and phi, but also to keep the advantages provided by brmsfit objects??</div><div><br></div><div>Thank you very much!</div><span><font color=\"#888888\"><div>Lucas</div><div><br></div></font></span></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><span><font color=\"#888888\">\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;brms-users&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">brms-users+unsubscribe@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo post to this group, send email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">brms-...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo view this discussion on the web visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/brms-users/5284cb61-9afd-43fb-a649-204ab44bf2fc%40googlegroups.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/brms-users/5284cb61-9afd-43fb-a649-204ab44bf2fc%2540googlegroups.com?utm_medium%3Demail%26utm_source%3Dfooter&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451331101000&amp;usg=AOvVaw07wtNz_tRzAH-PWoO-pApl\">https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/brms-users/5284cb61-9afd-43fb-a649-204ab44bf2fc%40googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451331101000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2tD5_JdyF1z6tsMZBdyTMn\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n</font></span></blockquote></div><br></div>\r\n"
    ]
