    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hello!<div><br></div><div>I&#39;m used to model jointly mean and dispersion for strictly positive variables. To do this, I describe response variable by a gamma distribution parametrized by the two orthogonal terms mu (mean) and phi (precision). That is, in stan code :</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">  shape </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">.*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">./</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  rate </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">./</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code><br style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\">     y ~ gamma(shape, rate); </span></div><div><br></div></div><div>Because I have to use splines to produce a GAMM for a particular problem, I found far more convenient to use brms instead of rstan. Unfortunatly, the gamma disribution is only parametrized in term of mu and shape, with the following formulation I found in the code created by brms : </div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><pre style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:19px;font-size:12.4pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><span style=\"outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> shape</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> exp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(-(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]));</span></font></span></pre></div></code></div><br>However, in that case, the precision is not independant of the mean.</div><div><br></div><div>Is there any trick that would allow me to use the formulation with mu and phi, but also to keep the advantages provided by brmsfit objects??</div><div><br></div><div>Thank you very much!</div><span><font color=\"#888888\"><div>Lucas</div><span><font color=\"#888888\"><div><br></div></font></span></font></span></div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div></blockquote></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
